  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + 0.5 z^2) / 2, drawn at 0.4 times its value, steeper along x. Plain steps overshoot along x above rate 0.4 and diverge above rate 0.8 (2 / 2.5). The start is x = 0.6, z = 1.7.",
    "Each step moves by -rate x (true slope + noise). The noise is random, centred on zero, with size 1.5 x sqrt((64 - b) / (63 b)) for batch b: largest at 1, exactly zero at 64.",
    "Both arrows are steps (not slopes) drawn at 5 times their true length, one scale for both. The purple one is the step the ball takes next; the dark one is the step the full data would give. The step-length readout is the true length.",
    "Diverged: the loss passed 5 or the ball left the drawn bowl; it is then drawn at the rim and the loss readout keeps its true value. A run also ends after 40 steps (finished).",
    "Noise comes from the fixed seed, the run number and the step, so Reset replays the same sequence, and each new run uses the next noise values.",
    "With noise a step lowers the loss only on average; single small-batch steps can raise it.",
    "The current trail and the kept path each hold the start plus at most 40 steps, one point per step."
  ]
}